1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,055
A type is a coarse promise.

2
00:00:02,055 --> 00:00:06,388
INTEGER says whole numbers, not that quantity is at least one.

3
00:00:06,388 --> 00:00:09,111
Constraints are the finer promises.

4
00:00:09,111 --> 00:00:12,180
NOT NULL means the fact must exist.

5
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,708
name TEXT NOT NULL.

6
00:00:13,708 --> 00:00:16,777
The engine refuses an insert that skips it.

7
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:20,500
DEFAULT fills a value when the insert omits the column.

8
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,555
DEFAULT zero for a counter.

9
00:00:22,555 --> 00:00:26,861
DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP for created_at.

10
00:00:26,861 --> 00:00:31,875
DEFAULT is not a substitute for NOT NULL if you still want the column present.

11
00:00:31,875 --> 00:00:33,000
They pair well.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,333
CHECK is a boolean test on the row.

13
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:37,791
CHECK quantity greater than zero.

14
00:00:37,791 --> 00:00:40,138
CHECK rating BETWEEN one AND five.

15
00:00:40,138 --> 00:00:43,430
CHECK price greater than or equal to zero.

16
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:46,013
The test may mention more than one column.

17
00:00:46,013 --> 00:00:50,680
CHECK end_on greater than or equal to start_on.

18
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:52,847
Keep CHECKs cheap and obvious.

19
00:00:52,847 --> 00:00:58,611
A CHECK that looks up another table is a foreign key, or a trigger, depending on the engine.

20
00:00:58,611 --> 00:01:01,694
SQLite CHECKs are real if you keep them on.

21
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:03,833
Some old environments skipped them.

22
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:04,972
Know your engine.

23
00:01:04,972 --> 00:01:06,402
Name the constraint.

24
00:01:06,402 --> 00:01:12,055
orders_quantity_positive is a better failure than CHECK constraint failed.

25
00:01:12,055 --> 00:01:15,472
Column constraints run on INSERT and UPDATE.

26
00:01:15,472 --> 00:01:18,680
They do not clean rows that were loaded with checks off.

27
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:22,166
VALIDATE after a bulk load if your dialect can.

28
00:01:22,166 --> 00:01:26,555
Do not encode a relationship in CHECK when a foreign key exists.

29
00:01:26,555 --> 00:01:30,722
Do not encode a unique rule in CHECK when UNIQUE exists.

30
00:01:30,722 --> 00:01:33,055
Use the tool that states the meaning.

31
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:38,097
NULL still bypasses most CHECKs, because unknown is not true.

32
00:01:38,097 --> 00:01:45,722
If zero is allowed and empty is not, NOT NULL plus CHECK quantity greater than or equal to zero.

33
00:01:45,722 --> 00:01:48,291
Write the promises in English first.

34
00:01:48,291 --> 00:01:49,527
Must exist.

35
00:01:49,527 --> 00:01:51,694
If omitted, use this.

36
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:53,291
Must pass this test.

37
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:57,527
Then NOT NULL, DEFAULT, CHECK fall out of the sentences.

38
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:04,027
CHECK (email LIKE %@%) is a weak email validator, and still better than nothing.

39
00:02:04,027 --> 00:02:07,000
Do not try to parse RFC email in CHECK.

40
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,944
Do it in the application or a real library, then keep a coarse CHECK.

41
00:02:11,944 --> 00:02:18,916
Exclusion constraints in PostgreSQL are a cousin: this range may not overlap that range.

42
00:02:18,916 --> 00:02:24,625
They are how rooms and time slots refuse double booking when UNIQUE is not enough.

43
00:02:24,625 --> 00:02:25,625
Know the name.

44
00:02:25,625 --> 00:02:27,333
Use it when you meet the room.

45
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:30,763
DEFAULT random() is a cute way to get a surprise.

46
00:02:30,763 --> 00:02:31,708
Do not.

47
00:02:31,708 --> 00:02:38,652
Defaults should be dull: zero, empty forbidden, now, a known status_id.

48
00:02:38,652 --> 00:02:42,555
A default that hides a missing fact will poison reports.

49
00:02:42,555 --> 00:02:49,694
If you do not know the city, NULL, not Unknown string, unless Unknown is a real place in your lookup table.

50
00:02:49,694 --> 00:02:53,666
Domain types in some engines bundle a type plus a CHECK.

51
00:02:53,666 --> 00:02:55,847
They are reusable promises.

52
00:02:55,847 --> 00:02:58,750
A column-level CHECK is enough while you learn.

53
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:04,833
Disable constraints only in a locked maintenance window, and re-enable with validation.

54
00:03:04,833 --> 00:03:09,222
A loader that needs them off is a loader that should write to staging instead.

55
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:11,166
Staging has loose rules.

56
00:03:11,166 --> 00:03:13,263
The real table has tight ones.

57
00:03:13,263 --> 00:03:16,500
Copy with a query that only moves clean rows.

58
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:20,111
The promises you wrote in English are the acceptance tests.

59
00:03:20,111 --> 00:03:22,666
NOT NULL, DEFAULT, CHECK.

60
00:03:22,666 --> 00:03:25,319
Three tools, a lot of quiet tables.

61
00:03:25,319 --> 00:03:27,694
Quiet tables make quiet nights.

62
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:29,708
A type lets 0 through.

63
00:03:29,708 --> 00:03:32,791
A CHECK decides whether 0 is a quantity.

64
00:03:32,791 --> 00:03:36,361
A DEFAULT decides what happens when the app is silent.

65
00:03:36,361 --> 00:03:39,111
NOT NULL decides whether silence is allowed.

66
00:03:39,111 --> 00:03:42,333
Four decisions, four tools if you count the type.

67
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:45,430
Skip one, and the table will invent a meaning.

68
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:50,027
Invented meanings show up in dashboards as mysterious zeros.

69
00:03:50,027 --> 00:03:52,736
Mysterious zeros become meetings.

70
00:03:52,736 --> 00:03:56,597
Meetings are more expensive than CHECK (quantity > 0).

71
00:03:56,597 --> 00:03:57,791
Name the CHECK.

72
00:03:57,791 --> 00:03:59,416
Refuse the mystery.

73
00:03:59,416 --> 00:04:02,055
English first, keywords second.

74
00:04:02,055 --> 00:04:06,694
The table will keep the promise at 2 a.m. when nobody is in the meeting.

75
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:08,805
That is the point of a constraint.

76
00:04:08,805 --> 00:04:15,625
NOT NULL, DEFAULT, and CHECK look optional until a NULL status means pending and failed at once.

77
00:04:15,625 --> 00:04:17,875
NULL is unknown or missing.

78
00:04:17,875 --> 00:04:29,805
Aggregates that meet a NULL can go quiet: SUM of a set containing NULL may ignore it, AVG too, while an expression like price plus tax becomes NULL if tax is NULL.

79
00:04:29,805 --> 00:04:32,291
Know the difference before you allow the hole.

80
00:04:32,291 --> 00:04:37,402
NOT NULL means the column will not accept NULL on INSERT or UPDATE.

81
00:04:37,402 --> 00:04:42,375
Username, password hash, and last name often cannot be holes.

82
00:04:42,375 --> 00:04:45,750
Phone might be a hole if the business does not require it.

83
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:52,024
People treat NOT NULL as a column property, like a type, so it is often unnamed.

84
00:04:52,024 --> 00:04:53,899
You still choose it on purpose.

85
00:04:53,899 --> 00:04:57,107
A primary key already implies NOT NULL.

86
00:04:57,107 --> 00:04:59,024
Saying it twice is noise.

87
00:04:59,024 --> 00:05:05,218
Saying it on email when email is UNIQUE is not noise if you refuse accounts without email.

88
00:05:05,218 --> 00:05:09,024
DEFAULT supplies a value when INSERT omits the column.

89
00:05:09,024 --> 00:05:11,718
It is not a check that the value is wise.

90
00:05:11,718 --> 00:05:17,302
DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP on created_at writes the clock you meant.

91
00:05:17,302 --> 00:05:21,302
DEFAULT zero on a quantity can hide a forgotten value.

92
00:05:21,302 --> 00:05:24,663
Choose defaults that are true history, not a shrug.

93
00:05:24,663 --> 00:05:32,774
A new NOT NULL column on a table that already has rows needs a default, or the ALTER fails, or old rows become lies.

94
00:05:32,774 --> 00:05:35,927
CHECK is a boolean test the row must pass.

95
00:05:35,927 --> 00:05:40,413
CHECK (price >= 0) stops a negative price.

96
00:05:40,413 --> 00:05:47,774
CHECK (status IN ('open', 'paid', 'canceled')) stops a status nobody defined.

97
00:05:47,774 --> 00:05:53,441
CHECK can compare two columns: end_date >= start_date.

98
00:05:53,441 --> 00:05:57,010
Keep CHECK expressions sargable and obvious.

99
00:05:57,010 --> 00:06:01,566
A check that calls a vague function nobody can read will not be maintained.

100
00:06:01,566 --> 00:06:03,232
Some engines skip CHECK.

101
00:06:03,232 --> 00:06:04,288
Know yours.

102
00:06:04,288 --> 00:06:06,538
NOT NULL is about presence.

103
00:06:06,538 --> 00:06:09,385
DEFAULT is about what presence is assumed.

104
00:06:09,385 --> 00:06:12,718
CHECK is about whether the present value is legal.

105
00:06:12,718 --> 00:06:16,246
Together they encode business rules next to the type.

106
00:06:16,246 --> 00:06:20,024
Application code still validates for friendly errors.

107
00:06:20,024 --> 00:06:26,788
The table validates for truth when a second writer appears, or a tool, or a tired INSERT.

108
00:06:26,788 --> 00:06:32,802
Ignore them and the database will store a negative salary with a NULL status and a smile.

109
00:06:32,802 --> 00:06:39,496
Name CHECK constraints so the error names the rule: orders_total_nonnegative.

110
00:06:39,496 --> 00:06:42,899
Then a failed write is a sentence, not a puzzle.

111
00:06:42,899 --> 00:06:47,441
Write the three on every column that has a rule in the interviews.

112
00:06:47,441 --> 00:06:52,913
If the interview never said the rule, do not invent a CHECK that will surprise the business.

113
00:06:52,913 --> 00:06:55,816
Constraints are requirements in SQL form.

114
00:06:55,816 --> 00:06:57,135
Not decoration.

115
00:06:57,135 --> 00:06:58,163
Not later.

116
00:06:58,163 --> 00:07:02,441
On the column, when you CREATE, while you still remember why.

117
00:07:02,441 --> 00:07:08,704
Revisit them when a rule changes, with ALTER, the same as any other contract.

118
00:07:08,704 --> 00:07:15,816
A status that gained a new legal value needs the CHECK rewritten, not a silent VARCHAR that already allowed it.

119
00:07:15,816 --> 00:07:19,468
That rewrite is cheaper than a year of fake statuses.

120
00:07:19,468 --> 00:07:29,454
Practice: take one table from the store schema and say for each column: hole or not, default or not, extra test or not.

121
00:07:29,454 --> 00:07:33,038
If you cannot answer, the requirement is unfinished.

122
00:07:33,038 --> 00:07:36,038
Finish the requirement, then the constraint.

123
00:07:36,038 --> 00:07:40,454
That is how NOT NULL, DEFAULT, and CHECK earn their keep.

124
00:07:40,454 --> 00:07:52,399
Dialect notes without turning into a vendor manual: some engines ignore CHECK, some allow multiple NULLs under UNIQUE, some attach NOT NULL as a property you cannot name.

125
00:07:52,399 --> 00:07:53,454
Read yours.

126
00:07:53,454 --> 00:07:59,524
The ideas stay: refuse holes, fill omissions, refuse illegal values.

127
00:07:59,524 --> 00:08:09,371
If CHECK is missing in an engine, you might use a trigger, which is a later tool, or you refuse in the application and accept the risk of a second writer.

128
00:08:09,371 --> 00:08:12,607
Prefer an engine that can CHECK if the rule matters.

129
00:08:12,607 --> 00:08:19,093
This course is fundamentals: the rule belongs next to the column whenever the engine can hold it.

130
00:08:19,093 --> 00:08:26,663
Walk DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE on an order_date and then INSERT an explicit old date for backfill.

131
00:08:26,663 --> 00:08:29,593
Explicit values override defaults.

132
00:08:29,593 --> 00:08:33,066
Defaults are for omission, not for locking the column.

133
00:08:33,066 --> 00:08:36,288
CHECK still validates the explicit old date.

134
00:08:36,288 --> 00:08:40,482
If backfill can be yesterday, the check must allow yesterday.

135
00:08:40,482 --> 00:08:46,454
If it cannot, the check and the backfill policy disagree, and you found a requirement bug.

136
00:08:46,454 --> 00:08:50,843
That is why this lecture is long: every keyword meets a policy.

137
00:08:50,843 --> 00:08:54,482
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

138
00:08:54,482 --> 00:08:57,538
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

139
00:08:57,538 --> 00:08:58,593
What is NULL?

140
00:08:58,593 --> 00:09:02,829
Unknown or missing, not zero, not empty string.

141
00:09:02,829 --> 00:09:04,579
What does NOT NULL refuse?

142
00:09:04,579 --> 00:09:06,677
Holes on INSERT and UPDATE.

143
00:09:06,677 --> 00:09:08,191
What does DEFAULT do?

144
00:09:08,191 --> 00:09:11,399
Fills a value when INSERT omits the column.

145
00:09:11,399 --> 00:09:13,746
Does DEFAULT block bad UPDATEs?

146
00:09:13,746 --> 00:09:14,510
No.

147
00:09:14,510 --> 00:09:15,607
CHECK does.

148
00:09:15,607 --> 00:09:16,843
What does CHECK do?

149
00:09:16,843 --> 00:09:19,441
A boolean test the row must pass.

150
00:09:19,441 --> 00:09:22,135
SUM versus a plus expression with NULL?

151
00:09:22,135 --> 00:09:25,371
SUM often skips NULL; plus becomes NULL.

152
00:09:25,371 --> 00:09:28,149
DEFAULT zero on unknown quantity?

153
00:09:28,149 --> 00:09:29,066
A lie.

154
00:09:29,066 --> 00:09:31,135
Zero is a real quantity.

155
00:09:31,135 --> 00:09:34,524
NOT NULL plus DEFAULT plus CHECK on status?

156
00:09:34,524 --> 00:09:37,677
Presence, starting value, legal list.

157
00:09:37,802 --> 00:09:39,238
New legal status?

158
00:09:39,238 --> 00:09:42,446
ALTER the CHECK, do not hope VARCHAR allows it.

159
00:09:42,446 --> 00:09:43,682
Why name CHECK?

160
00:09:43,682 --> 00:09:45,571
So the error names the rule.

161
00:09:45,571 --> 00:09:47,321
Do some engines skip CHECK?

162
00:09:47,321 --> 00:09:48,224
Yes.

163
00:09:48,224 --> 00:09:49,279
Know yours.

164
00:09:49,279 --> 00:09:51,210
Is NOT NULL usually named?

165
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:53,640
Often treated as a column property.

166
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:55,738
Primary key and NOT NULL?

167
00:09:55,738 --> 00:09:58,835
Primary key already implies NOT NULL.

168
00:09:58,835 --> 00:10:00,265
Test which writes?

169
00:10:00,265 --> 00:10:05,099
INSERT omit, INSERT illegal, UPDATE illegal, UPDATE NULL.

170
00:10:05,099 --> 00:10:07,821
Are the three keywords the same sentence?

171
00:10:07,821 --> 00:10:08,585
No.

172
00:10:08,585 --> 00:10:13,224
Required, if omitted use this, if present it must pass.

173
00:10:13,224 --> 00:10:14,377
Second writer?

174
00:10:14,377 --> 00:10:17,502
The table still enforces when an app does not.

175
00:10:17,502 --> 00:10:18,765
Negative salary?

176
00:10:18,765 --> 00:10:24,029
CHECK (salary >= 0), and NOT NULL if it must exist.

177
00:10:24,029 --> 00:10:26,085
created_at DEFAULT?

178
00:10:26,085 --> 00:10:28,265
A true clock, not a shrug.

179
00:10:28,265 --> 00:10:30,696
ALTER add NOT NULL to old rows?

180
00:10:30,696 --> 00:10:33,390
Need a default or fill the holes first.

181
00:10:33,390 --> 00:10:34,835
When do you skip a CHECK?

182
00:10:34,835 --> 00:10:37,988
Only with a reason the data owner would agree to.

183
00:10:37,988 --> 00:10:39,960
What is this lecture really about?

184
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:43,724
Business rules next to types, not optional decoration.

185
00:10:43,724 --> 00:10:45,293
Dialect differences?

186
00:10:45,293 --> 00:10:49,585
Read yours for CHECK and UNIQUE NULL, keep the three ideas.

187
00:10:49,585 --> 00:10:52,127
Explicit INSERT versus default?

188
00:10:52,127 --> 00:10:55,279
Explicit wins; CHECK still validates.

189
00:10:55,279 --> 00:10:57,029
Backfill versus CHECK?

190
00:10:57,029 --> 00:11:00,863
If they disagree, the requirement is unfinished.

191
00:11:00,863 --> 00:11:02,154
Three tests?

192
00:11:02,154 --> 00:11:05,543
NULL path, default path, illegal value.

193
00:11:05,543 --> 00:11:06,321
Goal?

194
00:11:06,321 --> 00:11:08,932
Boring columns that refuse nonsense.

195
00:11:08,932 --> 00:11:10,599
Optional constraints?

196
00:11:10,599 --> 00:11:12,682
How negative salaries arrived.

197
00:11:12,682 --> 00:11:13,807
When to write them?

198
00:11:13,807 --> 00:11:16,377
At CREATE, while you remember why.

199
00:11:16,377 --> 00:11:17,640
When to revisit?

200
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:20,085
When a rule changes, with ALTER.

201
00:11:20,085 --> 00:11:22,154
If the interview never said the rule?

202
00:11:22,154 --> 00:11:24,390
Do not invent a surprising CHECK.

203
00:11:24,390 --> 00:11:29,113
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

204
00:11:29,113 --> 00:11:50,265
Walk every column on orders: order_id PRIMARY KEY, customer_id NOT NULL REFERENCES, placed_at NOT NULL DEFAULT now, status NOT NULL DEFAULT open CHECK in the list, total DECIMAL NOT NULL CHECK greater than or equal to zero, notes TEXT NULL.

205
00:11:50,265 --> 00:11:53,488
That one table is the whole lecture in costume.

206
00:11:53,488 --> 00:11:56,043
order_id is identity.

207
00:11:56,043 --> 00:12:02,168
customer_id is presence plus a foreign key, which is next door's lesson.

208
00:12:02,168 --> 00:12:06,863
placed_at refuses holes and fills the clock when omitted.

209
00:12:06,863 --> 00:12:12,307
status refuses holes, starts open, refuses invented words.

210
00:12:12,307 --> 00:12:16,043
total refuses holes and refuses negative money.

211
00:12:16,043 --> 00:12:21,904
notes may be a hole, and empty string is a different choice you should not mix without a rule.

212
00:12:21,904 --> 00:12:26,724
INSERT that omits status becomes open, then CHECK still accepts open.

213
00:12:26,724 --> 00:12:31,085
UPDATE status to shipped fails until shipped is in the list.

214
00:12:31,085 --> 00:12:36,349
UPDATE total to minus one fails even though the row already existed.

215
00:12:36,349 --> 00:12:43,293
INSERT that omits notes stores NULL, and COUNT notes ignores that row, while COUNT star does not.

216
00:12:43,293 --> 00:12:47,252
That NULL lesson again, now attached to a real column.

217
00:12:47,252 --> 00:12:52,349
DEFAULT and CHECK together on status are why marketing cannot sneak a string.

218
00:12:52,349 --> 00:12:59,154
NOT NULL on total is why a forgotten total is not a silent NULL that poisons a plus tax expression.

219
00:12:59,154 --> 00:13:06,168
If tax is a separate column, decide NOT NULL DEFAULT zero versus NULL means unknown tax.

220
00:13:06,168 --> 00:13:08,710
Unknown tax cannot be invoiced.

221
00:13:08,710 --> 00:13:14,043
So NOT NULL DEFAULT zero may be honest for tax, and a lie for quantity.

222
00:13:14,043 --> 00:13:15,099
Say which.

223
00:13:15,099 --> 00:13:20,390
Write the CHECK that quantity is greater than zero if free gifts are stored another way.

224
00:13:20,390 --> 00:13:28,377
If free gifts exist, quantity greater than or equal to zero, and a gift flag, or a zero-price product.

225
00:13:28,377 --> 00:13:30,043
The CHECK is the policy.

226
00:13:30,043 --> 00:13:37,460
Eighteen minutes is policies meeting keywords, over and over, until you can do it cold on a new table.

227
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:49,807
Do it cold on reviews next: review_id, product_id NOT NULL, body NOT NULL, rating CHECK between one and five, created_at DEFAULT now.

228
00:13:49,807 --> 00:13:56,682
If rating can be skipped, allow NULL and do not DEFAULT three, because three is a real opinion.

229
00:13:56,682 --> 00:14:01,043
That is the last mile of NOT NULL, DEFAULT, and CHECK.

230
00:14:01,043 --> 00:14:03,474
You are not recapping three words.

231
00:14:03,474 --> 00:14:08,807
You are refusing fake ratings, fake totals, and fake statuses at the door.

